  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nLow overcast is hanging with us today amid a warm advective\nlayer about 925mb. Satellite shows drier air/better mixing just\nsoutheast of the area and that should start to work into western\nKentucky and spread the rest of the area through late afternoon\nbut some overcast will likely persist across SEMO/Southern\nIllinois much of the night.\n\nThe main focus of the current forecast is the very large and\ndeep trough ejecting off the Rocky Mountains in NM/CO this\nafternoon. That trough is expected to continue to be booted\neastward by 100-110kt jet behind it. This swings the trough to a\nnegative tilt as it moves into the southern Plains and mid-\nMississippi Valley tomorrow afternoon. The jet-level ascent\npicture develops to provide a parent surface low well to the\nnorth but also would generate some troughing or a secondary low\nnear the area or perhaps slightly south to southeast of us in\nthe evening.\n\nThere is definitely some degree of severe thunderstorm potential\nwith the passage of the front in the afternoon and early\nevening. Instability is the main limiting factor. Mid-level\nlapse rates are quite poor owing to the moist/semi-\ntropical airmass being dragged in ahead of the system. Low level\nmoisture return is limited by the southeast winds initally\ndrawing from relatively dry air over MS/AL. Dewpoints do work\ninto the low to mid 60s in most available guidance as richer\nmoisture eventually works into the low levels. This yields about\n300-400 J/kg of entrainment factored CAPE as convection moves\ninto the area. \n\n0-6km shear is about 40-50 knots with 850mb flow\nincreasing to 40 to 45 kts as well giving a pretty large \nhodograph curvature. I am a little concerned the strong large\nscale ascent from the negative tilt trough could cause lapse\nrates to be steeper than modeled, although it would have a lot\nto overcome to make for an appreciable increase in instability.\nI expect convection may struggle and only be pseudo-surface\nbased but the shear will be strong enough we will have to watch\nvery closely through the day for any breakthrough severe risk\nwith tornado and wind the main concern. Will need to watch obs\ntrends and future CAM runs for any trends that might suggest\nthings going one way or the other. The background winds will be\nstrong and gusty 25-35 mph but for now with limited mixing I \nthink the odds of advisory level gusts (40 mph) are too low.\nLocally heavy rainfall looks probable but the system will\nprobably be progressive enough given the dry antecedent\nconditions to prevent a significant flood risk.\n\nBehind the front cooler and relatively stable conditions with\nseasonable temperatures look to set up shop. Another front looks\npossible Sunday that seems to have a shot of colder air behind\nit that might result in overnight frost/freeze conditions.",
